{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-masquerade/1462900-review-walk-among-us","id":1462900,"title":"[Review] Walk Among Us","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Vampire: The Masquerade"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-10-06T10:39:00-07:00","id":1462900,"title":"[Review] Walk Among Us","content":"I see everyone's talking about the new Sabbat book, or the comics, or the video games, but no one is discussing the new prose collection, so I thought I'd kick things off. <i>Walk Among Us</i> is a collection of three novellas, and I'll be reviewing and discussing them as I read them. Feel free to ask questions and post your thoughts if you've read them.<br /> <br /> Also, I see the internet really only returns search results for the audiobook version, which is strange. I borrowed my copy from the library in print, so check you local libraries - they may have it available in either form.<br /> <br /> The first story is &quot;A Sheep Among Wolves&quot; by Genevieve Gornichec. This is pretty standard fare - a revenge story, simple plot twists, ultimately cathartic in the same sense as young-adult* or introductory fiction. I am not familiar with the author, and find her writing style serviceable, if in need of a little work. The most jarring aspects being the blatant product placement (Nintendo Switch, Raising Cane's Chicken, etc.), which pins and dates a story in a very specific sense of time. The text, and the author, almost feel obligated to force <i>Vampire</i> terminology, with a character at one point literally proclaiming &quot;We're the Anarchs!&quot;, which feels like it should have a large \u00a9 right after it. Needs must, to be included in the collection, I assume.<br /> <br /> Final review: mostly inoffensive, somewhat bland, slightly juvenile, but not terrible. B-<br /> <br /> Cheers!<br /> <br /> *The is nothing wrong with enjoying young-adult fiction.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you don't use an Oxford comma, I feel bad for you, son,<br /> 'Cuz I got ninety-nine problems, but clarity ain't one.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/408-nothing","name":"nothing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar408_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"572"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-06T12:17:00-07:00","id":1462914,"title":"","content":"Not Caine's Chicken?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/490-omenseer","name":"omenseer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar490_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"987"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-06T13:02:49-07:00","id":1462923,"title":"","content":"I enjoyed the plot twist at the end of the first novella. The main characters arc is pretty strong which i enjoyed. I feel like it did a good job of slowly introducing you to the basics of kindred politics but yeah they did toss in a lot of jargon near the end.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3901-radiofreedeath","name":"RadioFreeDeath","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"177"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-10-11T10:32:42-07:00","id":1463436,"title":"","content":"All right, time for the second novella, &quot;Fine Print&quot; by Cassandra Khaw. This story is more in line with with the product in terms of theme and atmosphere, but it is also... disquieting. It is, frankly, a little vulgar. The protagonist is unlikable, none of the other characters get enough depth for one to form an opinion, the events are repugnant, but - and this is a fair warning - it does track and examine the monotonousness of Kindred un-life. It does it very well. &quot;A beast I am...&quot; and all that, the novella showcases what happens when one continues to believe that vampires are still human. The author's writing style started a little verbose, purple prose and ten-cent words in a penny dreadful, but after finishing my read, I feel that the language helps to identify the haughty, arrogant attitude of the protagonist (and the vocabulary becomes more serviceable as the tale progresses).<br /> <br /> Final review: Definite content warning, strays into that 90s &quot;grimdark&quot; at a few points, but shines in some places. Solid B<br /> <br /> Cheers!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you don't use an Oxford comma, I feel bad for you, son,<br /> 'Cuz I got ninety-nine problems, but clarity ain't one.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/408-nothing","name":"nothing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar408_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"572"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-02T11:50:50-07:00","id":1466307,"title":"","content":"After some computer issues, I'm back with a review of the final story, &quot;The Land of Milk and Honey&quot; by Caitlin Starling. This is the style of story I wanted - personal and political horror, and solidly written. The lexicon is unobtrusive - terms from the game flow naturally in service of the story. The variety of characters are interesting, and the core conceit, while trite, fuels an intriguing and engaging series of events. While not perfect, this is the story I would recommend for everyone to read. Final grade: A<br /> <br /> Cheers!","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you don't use an Oxford comma, I feel bad for you, son,<br /> 'Cuz I got ninety-nine problems, but clarity ain't one.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/408-nothing","name":"nothing","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar408_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"572"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-11-02T18:22:37-07:00","id":1466365,"title":"","content":"Pretty much agree on your ratings for each story!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/27090-jonny","name":"Jonny","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2021"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"60"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}